2509.01412,Vis-CoT: A Human-in-the-Loop Framework for Interactive Visualization and Intervention in LLM Chain-of-Thought Reasoning,"Large language models (LLMs) show strong reasoning via chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting, but the process is opaque, which makes verification, debugging, and control difficult in high-stakes settings. We present Vis-CoT, a human-in-the-loop framework that converts linear CoT text into an interactive reasoning graph. Users can visualize the logical flow, identify flawed steps, and intervene by pruning incorrect paths and grafting new, user-defined premises. This shifts interaction from passive observation to active collaboration, steering models toward more accurate and trustworthy conclusions. Across GSM8K and StrategyQA, Vis-CoT improves final-answer accuracy by up to 24 percentage points over non-interactive baselines. A user study also shows large gains in perceived usability and trust. Vis-CoT points to a practical path for more reliable, understandable, and collaborative reasoning by combining LLMs with targeted human oversight.",2025,0,"['Kaviraj Pather', 'E. Hadjigeorgiou', 'Arben Krasniqi', 'C. Schmit', 'I. Rusu', 'M. Pons', 'Kabir Khan']",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.01412.pdf,,True,"findings, their implications, and the limitations of our work.
A. Quantitative Performance Analysis
The primary goal of our evaluation was to determine whether
interactive intervention could lead to superior task outcomes com-
pared to non-interactive methods. The results, summarized in Table I,
demonstrate a significant improvement in final answer accuracy
across all three datasets when users were equipped with the Vis-CoT
framework.
On the GSM8K dataset, Vis-CoT achieved an accuracy of 91.7%,
a substantial 16.9 percentage point increase over the Standard CoT
baseline. Our analysis of user sessions revealed that the majority
of errors in the Standard CoT were due to single-step arithmetic
mistakes or misinterpretations of the problem’s constraints. The
Vis-CoT interface enabled users to quickly spot and correct these
localized errors using a simple ”Grafting” operation, preventing the
entire reasoning chain from being derailed. This ability to make
precise, surgical corrections stands in contrast to methods that rely
on the model to self-correct [45], which may not always identify its
own subtle flaws.
The performance gain was similarly pronounced for StrategyQA,
where Vis-CoT reached 94.1% accuracy. Here, errors often stemmed
from factual inaccuracies or logical fallacies in the reasoning chain.
Users leveraged the framework to prune entire fallacious sub-trees
and graft new steps supported by their own external knowledge,
effectively guiding the LLM away from plausible but incorrect
narratives. This highlights the synergy between the LLM’s generative
power and human critical thinking.
The most dramatic improvement was observed in our custom
Multi-Step Planning Task, where Vis-CoT’s accuracy of 92.0% more
than doubled the Zero-Shot baseline and was 24 points higher than
Standard CoT. These tasks, with their complex inter-dependencies,
are particularly vulnerable to cascading errors. The global graph view
provided by Vis-CoT (as conceptualized in Fig. 1) was instrumental,
allowing users to maintain a high-level overview of the entire plan
while intervening on specific details. This holistic view is critical for
complex problem-solving, a principle that also applies to developing
robust, context-aware systems in other domains, such as multi-modal
emotion recognition .
We also analyzed the efficiency of the human-in-the-loop process,
with results shown in Table II. While Vis-CoT required a longer
average completion time for problems the Standard CoT model solved
correctly on its first attempt, it was significantly more efficient when
the initial solution was flawed. The Standard CoT method forced
users into a time-consuming cycle of full regenerations, whereas
Vis-CoT allowed for rapid, targeted fixes. The average number of
interventions per task was modest (2.1), indicating that users did not
feel overwhelmed and could achieve correct results with only a few
key adjustments. This efficiency is crucial for practical applications,
where user time is a valuable resource. The challenge of optimizing
for efficiency while maintaining high performance is a common
theme in machine learning, from designing efficient temporal filters
for video grounding to developing streamlined model compression
techniques .
B. Qualitative Findings and User Study Insights
Beyond quantitative performance, we sought to understand the
user experience of interacting with Vis-CoT. The results from our
qualitative assessments were overwhelmingly positive, as shown in
Table III.
The average SUS score for Vis-CoT was 88.2, falling into the
”Excellent” category, whereas the Standard CoT baseline scored a
”Marginal” 65.5. This significant difference was echoed in the post-
task interviews. A common theme was the sense of agency and
control that Vis-CoT provided. One participant remarked, ”With the
standard interface, if the answer was wrong, it felt like hitting a
brick wall. With Vis-CoT, a wrong step felt like a starting point for
a collaboration.” This sentiment captures the shift from a passive
consumer of AI outputs to an active partner in the problem-solving
process.
This increased sense of control directly translated to higher trust.
The Trust in AI score for Vis-CoT was 4.6 out of 5, compared to
a neutral 2.8 for the baseline. Users reported feeling much more
confident in the final answers they arrived at using Vis-CoT because
