A
More Related Works
A.1
Large Vision-Language Models
Vision-Language Models (VLMs) that couple visual encoders with large language models have
rapidly advanced general-purpose multimodal understanding [22], with CLIP [23] establishing
scalable vision-language representations for strong zero-shot transfer. Proprietary models like
GPT-4o [24] and Gemini [25] push multimodal reasoning at scale, while open-weight models such as
DeepSeek [26] and Qwen-VL [53] lower the barrier for domain adaptation in specialized applications.
Adapting VLMs to agriculture shows promise but also reveals gaps. Domain-specific initiatives
include AgroGPT [32] for agricultural VQA, Agri-LLaVA [33] for crop disease diagnosis, and vision-
language pipelines for crop health monitoring, disease recognition [3] or parcel segmentation [9].
However, these models are often trained and evaluated on crop- or symptom-specific datasets with
limited scenes and sensors [15, 3], and current evaluations rarely cover practical skills such as
counting, planting recommendations or environmental management. Therefore, there is an urgent
need for a benchmark that spans a broader range of agricultural scenes across multiple sensing
platforms, so that the capabilities of models can be assessed more faithfully in real-world field
conditions.
A.2
Chain-of-Thought
Chain-of-Thought (CoT) [28] helps large models solve complex problems by breaking them into
intermediate steps. Simple prompts can elicit stepwise reasoning and improve performance on
arithmetic, commonsense, and logic tasks [17, 27, 38]. Reliability is further enhanced through
self-consistency voting [56] and systematic search over intermediate states [57]. In multimodal
settings, textual rationales should align with visual evidence [29], as in ScienceQA [21], which pairs
images, questions, and human-written rationales to supervise step-by-step reasoning. Compositional
CoT [30] uses scene-graph intermediates, and Interleaved-modal CoT [31] inserts attention-selected
regions.
Despite this progress, multimodal CoT benchmarks [58–62, 38, 19] remain limited in reasoning
depth, image diversity, and domain coverage. ScienceQA [21] targets K–12; MMMU [36] and
MathVista [35] emphasize cross-disciplinary and visual mathematics without explicit long-chain
annotations; A-OKVQA [34] offers training or multiple-choice rationales but does not evaluate
generated intermediate steps at scale; M3CoT [37] and MME-CoT [38] enforce multi-domain, multi-
step, multimodal reasoning but do not cover agriculture. This reveals a core limitation for agricultural
multimodal reasoning: the absence of a benchmark with detailed CoT for qualitative assessment.
Motivated by this, we introduce explicit, structured CoT for agricultural scenarios, which enables
evaluation of the CoT generated by models and provides deeper insights into how well models
understand and analyze complex agricultural problems.
B
Data Collection
B.1
Data Sources Selection
AgroCoT is constructed based on four state-of-the-art agricultural vision-language benchmarks
(see Table 4), each offering unique characteristics and specialized annotations for comprehensive
evaluation of VLMs in agriculture.
IP102 [45] is a large-scale benchmark dataset for insect pest recognition. It contains diverse pest cate-
gories with fine-grained visual differences, making it useful for evaluating agricultural vision models
on pest identification and long-tailed category recognition. In AgroCoT, IP102 mainly contributes
to pest-related visual understanding, including insect recognition, pest category discrimination, and
reasoning over visually similar agricultural threats.
Agriculture-Vision Corpus (AVC) [44] is a large aerial image dataset designed for agricultural
pattern analysis. It provides high-resolution field imagery and supports the recognition of farmland
patterns, crop conditions, and abnormal agricultural regions. In AgroCoT, AVC enriches aerial-view
agricultural understanding and contributes to tasks related to field-scene interpretation, anomaly
identification, and remote observation of farmland conditions.
